Greedy decoding inflates depth separability: Pythia-6.9B yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ under greedy but $\Kempirical{=}1$ under nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$), with the borderline 1v2 score (2 of 5 folds above $\theta$) illustrating that the boundary is continuous rather than a cliff (Table~\ref{tab:s10_pythia_decoding}; Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta}).

\paragraph{What $\Kempirical$ reveals.}
$\Kempirical$ establishes why binary detection suffices for web text (depth${>}1$ is invisible to statistical features), identifies domains where finer strategies are viable (arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$), and quantifies how scale, scorer, and threshold jointly shape the boundary.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$.
One-wash laundering refers to this concentrated signal loss at the 0v1 boundary: while depth-1 remains marginally distinguishable from depth-2 (1v2 $\auc$ 0.61--0.70, the $\Kempirical{=}2$ finding), the dominant distributional shift occurs at generation $0{\to}1$, and beyond depth~2 no further signal is exploitable.
Converging evidence supports a data-intrinsic interpretation: OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 8--38\% (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).
One-wash laundering is distinct from model collapse~\cite{shumailov2024collapse}: model collapse describes cumulative quality degradation across generations, while one-wash laundering describes the erasure of statistical fingerprints distinguishing adjacent generations; text quality may continue degrading even after the features separating generation $k$ from $k{+}1$ become indistinguishable.

\paragraph{Representation dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia yields $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ and OLMo yields $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 1$, consistent with its 15D boundary (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).
The 15D features trade one depth level of sensitivity for cross-model consistency (6/10 configurations $\Kempirical{=}2$), making them the preferred operational choice.
The gap between $\Kempirical$ and $\Ktheory$ remains open; a DeBERTa-v3-base upper-bound probe yields $\Kempirical^{\text{DeBERTa}} = \Kempirical^{\text{OBD}} = 2$ on C4 and Code but $\Kempirical^{\text{DeBERTa}} = 4$ on arXiv, placing the cost of generator-agnostic features at one depth level for structured text and zero for web text (Table~\ref{tab:deberta_vs_obd}).
Feature rankings are scorer-independent ($\rho > 0.90$); $\Kempirical$ agreement is scorer-independent for strong-signal configurations (e.g., Qwen-rewrite retains $\Kempirical{=}2$ under all scorers) but scorer-sensitive at marginal boundaries (Qwen-continuation drops to $\Kempirical{=}1$; length confound ruled out in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
Self-scoring inflates pairwise AUC: OLMo shows the largest gap (1v2 $\Delta$ = +11.5\,pp; self-scored $\Kempirical$=2 vs.\ cross-scored $\Kempirical$=1), followed by Pythia (+4.7\,pp) and Mistral-7B (+2.4\,pp).
Cross-scoring provides a conservative, deployment-realistic estimate of $K^*$, as real-world contamination originates from unknown generators.

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
Web text (C4, Wikipedia) and Code (Python) consistently yield $\Kempirical{=}2$, while structured academic text (arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ across three independent architecture families (Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}), as formulaic structure (citations, math notation, section conventions) creates larger per-generation distributional shifts (1v2 JSD $= 0.077$ vs.\ ${\sim}0.01$ for C4).
This elevation is not driven by \LaTeX{} syntax: stripping all markup preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ (2v3 $\Delta\auc = {-}0.021$).
Code (Python) provides an informative contrast: despite rigid syntactic structure, it yields $\Kempirical{=}2$, confirming that the arXiv elevation reflects academic conventions rather than structural complexity per se.
$\Kempirical$ varies by domain, suggesting that any future depth-aware filtering should account for source characteristics rather than assuming a universal boundary.

\paragraph{Prompt-chain scope and retrain-chain gap.}
Retrain-chain experiments across three families (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, Qwen-1.5B; LoRA) yield $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (3v4 $\auc = 0.68$--$0.80$); extending Pythia and Qwen to depth~7 sustains $\Kempirical{=}7$ (6v7 $\auc = 0.634$--$0.641 > \theta$), far exceeding the prompt-chain boundary because each retrain generation also modifies model parameters, introducing cumulative adaptation artifacts absent from text-only recycling.
Prompt-chain $\Kempirical$ isolates text-intrinsic degradation, the operationally relevant quantity for data curation pipelines; our conclusions do not directly transfer to retrain-chain (model collapse) settings.

\paragraph{Boundary cases and scale effect.}
$\Kempirical{=}1$ configurations cluster at 1v2 $\auc$ 0.500--0.598, well separated from the $\Kempirical{=}2$ range (0.611--0.696); $\Kempirical{=}1$ is a text property (these models produce first-generation output so human-like that statistical features cannot distinguish depth~1 from depth~2), not a classifier failure.
Larger models reduce 0v1 JSD (0.077 at 8B vs.\ 0.15--0.27 at $\leq$1.5B), suggesting 13B+ may increasingly yield $\Kempirical{=}1$.

\paragraph{Decoding strategy as confound.}
Greedy decoding inflates depth separability: Pythia-6.9B yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ under greedy but $\Kempirical{=}1$ under nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$), with the borderline 1v2 score (2 of 5 folds above $\theta$) illustrating that the boundary is continuous rather than a cliff (Table~\ref{tab:s10_pythia_decoding}; Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta}).

\paragraph{What $\Kempirical$ reveals.}
$\Kempirical$ establishes why binary detection suffices for web text (depth${>}1$ is invisible to statistical features), identifies domains where finer strategies are viable (arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$), and quantifies how scale, scorer, and threshold jointly shape the boundary.

